const PATTERNS = [
  ["A", "Direct collection, conversion and accumulation", `The everyday deal. A sender pays a partner, the partner converts the money, and the converted money waits at the partner until it is paid out. The fee is taken at the moment of conversion, and that is the only moment when some of the client's money becomes NPL's.`, ["T1", "T2", "T3a", "T3"]],
  ["B", "Part collection", `A sender may pay a deal in several instalments. Each instalment is recorded as it arrives; the conversion waits until the deal is fully paid.`, ["T4", "T5", "T6"]],
  ["C", "Settlement, confirmation, bank fees, shortfalls, rounding", `Paying out: the partner sends the money, and from that moment it is out of NPL's hands, so the promise is treated as kept. The receiver's confirmation changes nothing unless it is short; a short delivery or a return brings the promise back for the missing part. Bank fees and rounding differences are settled according to the rules of the project. Bank transfers go out exact, cents included; rounding to a unit applies to cash hand-overs.`, ["T7", "T8", "T9", "T10", "T11"]],
  ["D", "Contingency routes and the bridge wallet", `Sometimes money has to pass through one of NPL's own wallets: when the usual partner is unavailable, or when the sender can only pay on a network the partner does not accept. The ledger tracks it there until it leaves.`, ["T12", "T13", "T14", "T15", "T16", "T17"]],
  ["E", "Cancellations, reversals and voids", `Two different things happen here. <b>A mistake in the books</b> (T18, T19): a posting was wrong, say a collection booked against the wrong deal. The ledger never lets anyone edit or delete it, because then yesterday's reports would no longer add up. Instead a second posting is made that is the exact mirror of the first and points at it, so the two cancel, and then the correct posting is made. Three entries stay on record: the wrong one, its cancellation, the right one. <b>A cancelled deal</b> (T20, T21): the money really did arrive, but the deal is called off, so the money has to go back to the sender. The promise to deliver is torn up and the money leaves the partner. Sending crypto costs a network fee; the project's rules say whether the sender bears it (the refund is a little smaller) or NPL does (the refund is whole and NPL books the fee as its cost). Because money has already moved, Management must approve how a cancelled deal is unwound.`, ["T18", "T19", "T20", "T21"]],
  ["E+", null, `Every other kind of mistake is fixed the same way as T19: a mirror-image posting that points at the wrong one, then the right posting, both dated today. A posting made twice, money sent to the wrong receiver, a conversion booked in the wrong currency, a correction of only part of an amount, a mistake found after the month has been reported to the accountant: each is a reversal and a re-posting, labelled with the month it belongs to when that month has already been reported. The worked examples will be added when the first real case happens during the parallel run.`, []],
  ["F", "Rejected payout and return leg", `A bank can reject a payout. The money comes back, the failed payout is undone, and the client can either try again later or have the money converted back and returned.`, ["T22a", "T22", "T23", "T24", "T24a", "T25"]],
  ["H", "Balance conversion", `Money already converted and waiting at a partner can be converted again into another currency, without a new sender or receiver, when a payout needs it.`, ["T26", "T27"]],
  ["I", "Overpayment and sender credit", `A sender who pays too much gets a credit, which is refunded or used on the next deal.`, ["T28", "T29"]],
  ["J", "Markup shares and partner rebates", `Two kinds of side payments, recorded the moment the conversion happens and settled monthly against a statement. A share is part of NPL's fee owed to a markup-share party — a customer such as Raeen, or an outside introducer; a rebate is money a partner gives back to NPL, kept separate and never shared.`, ["T30", "T31", "T32", "T33", "T34", "T35"]],
  ["K", "Variances, by kind", `The client's figures are fixed when the sender is quoted. Whenever the partner's actual conversion differs from the one the quote assumed, the difference is NPL's, and it is filed in one of four jars according to why it happened.`, ["T36", "T37", "T38"]],
  ["L", "Loss events: booked to NPL first, recovered share by share", `When money is lost after it was verified (a frozen account, cash gone missing), NPL takes the whole loss on itself at once and makes the client whole. Then, as each party agrees to bear a share, that share is recovered.`, ["T39", "T40", "T41a", "T41b", "T41c", "T42", "T43"]],
  ["M", "The reseller project (NPL-GR): two invoices, one remittance deal", `NPL-GR buys goods from a vendor and resells them. Its profit is the difference between the vendor's invoice and its own resale invoice, both in euros, fixed up front. The customer pays through NPL's remittance service, which charges its own 2 % and absorbs all the currency ups and downs so the reseller profit is never touched.`, ["T44", "T45", "T46", "T47", "T48", "T49", "T50", "T51"]],
  ["N", "Earnings kept in the unconverted balance and offset between partners", `Partners convert everything they receive, so NPL's fee normally ends up converted along with the client's money. NPL prefers to keep its fees in USDT, taken out of money that has not been converted yet, even when the fee was earned at a different partner. The ledger records that as a swap between NPL and the client at the day's rate.`, ["T52a", "T52", "T53", "T54", "T55"]],
  ["O", "Reroute after conversion", `Sometimes a partner has already turned the client's money into euros and then cannot pay them out: it is suspended, its bank is down, a limit is hit, or it is a holiday. NPL decided on 20 October that in that case the money does not simply wait. It is moved to another partner: the first partner turns the euros back into USDT at its rate of the day, sends the USDT to NPL's own wallet, NPL forwards it to the second partner, and the second partner turns it into euros again at its own rate. The client is promised exactly what it was promised before; whatever the round trip costs or gains is NPL's. Management must approve, because for a while NPL's own wallet is holding the client's money, which is the fourth of the cases in which that is allowed.`, ["T56", "T57", "T58"], staticBalancesHtml(BAL_O, "Balances after Pattern O")],
  ["P", "Interruptions between instruction and recording", `What happens when something breaks in the middle: the partner has done its part but NPLify did not record it, a receiver never says the money arrived, or the blockchain explorer is down. The rule that keeps the books safe is simple: no automatic process ever moves value between jars. A person records what happened, once, with the evidence, and the system refuses to record the same event twice. So a breakdown can make a posting late; it can never make it double. The three cases and how each is resolved are set out in Pattern P of the Ledger Posting Design; the worked postings are added once the real behaviour has been exercised on staging.`, []],
  ["Q", "Month-end reconciliation with a partner statement", `Once a month, and every evening during the parallel run, Finance puts the partner's statement next to the ledger. For each partner and currency the partner holds one balance; NPL's books say the same money is partly the client's jar and partly NPL's pool jar, and the two jars must add up to the partner's figure. When they do not, Finance finds out why before anything is posted: a payout the partner has already sent but the receiver has not yet confirmed (a timing difference, nothing to post), a charge the partner took and did not advise (a true-up, below), or a mistake on the partner's side (a query to the partner, nothing to post). Each reconciliation is kept as a record with the figures, the cause and who resolved it.`, ["T59"]],
  ["R", "Opening entries on the opening date", `On the day NPLify takes over, the money is already where it is: the client's euros are waiting at Ali, NPL's fees are resting there too, a rebate is owed by Jeton, a sender has a credit. Rather than re-typing months of old deals to arrive at those figures, Finance lists every such balance from the sheets, checks each one against the partner's statement or the wallet, Management approves the list, and each line is posted once as an opening entry with the statement attached. The jars start full, and everything that happens afterwards is an ordinary posting on top.${mk(" The list is never a lump sum per partner: one line per receiver group and per sender, so the entitlement of each group and the balance of each sender start right; Finance adds the lines up three ways — per partner against the partner's statement, per group against the sheet, per sender against the sheet — before Management approves, and a line put under the wrong group shows up in the second and third sums even though the partner total still agrees.")}`, ["T60", "T61"]],
];

const STACK_AT = 6; // tables with this many lines or more get the explanation on top
const pair = (key) => {
  const t = L.txs[L.find(key)];
  const stacked = t.rows.length >= STACK_AT;
  const expl = X[key]; if (!expl) throw new Error("no explanation for " + key);
  const table = L.html(key).replace(/<p class="txnote">[\s\S]*?<\/p>/, "");
  const nums = N[key] ? `<div class="gh" style="margin-top:5pt">The arithmetic</div><ul class="nums">${N[key].map((l) => `<li>${l}</li>`).join("")}</ul>` : "";
  return `<div class="pair${stacked ? " stack" : ""}"><div class="left">${table}</div><div class="right"><div class="guide"><div class="gh">What is happening</div>${expl}${nums}</div></div></div>`;
};

